  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nBy Saturday a second follow-on shortwave trough will quickly move\nin from the northwest as early as sunrise Saturday morning, \nlasting through at least Saturday afternoon. Compared to the short\nwave on Friday, the track of this next disturbance will remain \nacross northern AZ, bringing rain chances to at least the northern\nhalf of Arizona with more doubt for the Arizona lower deserts. \nSimilarly to Friday, rainfall amounts will again be light, \nhowever slightly higher in comparison, especially in the higher \nterrain where amounts between 0.10\"-0.25\" are likely.\n\nBy Saturday evening, the shortwave is expected to push to our\neast quickly decreasing rain chances and likely ending them all\ntogether by around midnight with Sunday slotted to be a down day \nfor shower activity with a brief period of shortwave ridging and \nsome slight dry air advection from the northwest and should lead \nto considerably less clouds and virtually no rain chances.\nTemperatures will continue to be at to slightly below normal over\nthe weekend and heading into next week. \n\nModels continue to forecast one after another northern Pacific \nwaves being ejected southeastward toward the U.S. West Coast with \nthe next potential system reaching the California coast early \nMonday before reaching our region Monday night into Tuesday. This \nmay even be followed by another system during the second half of \nnext week. However, it is too early to have any good sense on the \ntrack of these systems as they could move directly through the \nDesert Southwest or mostly miss us to the north, so continued \nmonitoring will be needed. NBM/WPC PoPs of 20-60% are still in \nthe forecast for the Tuesday disturbance, but that could easily \nchange depending on the eventual track. So far, any system that \nmay move through is not expected to carry anything more than \naverage moisture, so any rainfall amounts are likely to be on the \nlighter side. However, the expected northwestward trajectory of \nthe weather systems next week should usher in even cooler \ntemperatures with highs trending more toward the upper 60s to \nlower 70s during the middle part of the week.",
